From 4d8d4b181ad6f8e7f61a27324224f70165ad07ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 17:17:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] [DOSKUTSU] patch 0294 -- CPU witness: attest the running
 silicon at boot

Nothing in any log witnessed which CPU ran a benchmark cell. Video is provable
from evidence (vram=4096KB LFB vs 1024KB banked) and sound from is_sb16/dsp_ver/
highdma, but the CPU was identified ONLY by a digit the operator typed into a
sweep BAT, which merely set the log-tag prefix. Every cross-CPU conclusion in the
campaign rested on that one unverifiable keystroke.

Attest it in-engine at boot. Emit both identities: CPUID vendor + family/model/
stepping is exact, free, and by itself separates Pentium (GenuineIntel family 5)
from Am5x86 (AuthenticAMD family 4) from Intel 486; then a MHz estimate mirrored
from setup/profile.c estimate_mhz() -- TSC-exact on Pentium, calibrated busy-loop
on 486 with MHZ_486_LOOP_DIV=16400 UNCHANGED so engine numbers are directly
comparable to SETUP's -- splits DX2-66 from DX2-50 within the 486 family. CPUID
absence (early 486 steppings) falls back to the timing loop alone. cpu_class
buckets (586 / 486_mid / 486_slow) mirror profile.c detect_cpu().

The probe runs once at main() entry, after Logger::init (the banner needs it) and
before SDL_Init and any flip, so the ~220 ms calibration window is invisible to
the fps measurement. Default-ON with a strict killswitch
SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_CPU_WITNESS=0 (getenv, pre-SDL_Init) as fault insurance for any
machine on which the boot-time timing loop misbehaves.

Also folds the in-lane hardware witnesses the engine reliably knows into the
manifest -- audio_backend (from the sticky backend enum) and the locked logical
video mode -- so analysis stops grepping a second log for them. The SDL-probe
card-identity witnesses (video vram_kb / LFB-vs-banked, audio is_sb16 / dsp_ver /
dma_path) still live in <TAG>SDL.LOG and need SDL getter exports to consolidate;
that is a follow-up, and nothing is lost meanwhile.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
